# 0907 — 2022-09-07, glibc 11.1.0 and uclibc 9.1.0

## Release

    release_0907   built 2022-09-07
    project_commit 3a7aec9
    sdk_commit     3dcd67a
    mhal_commit    a7acd5c
    vermagic       5.10.61 preempt mod_unload ARMv7 thumb2 p2v8

Taken from the vendor SDK release tree, not harvested from a shipped firmware
and not taken from OpenIPC. Stronger provenance than the 1230 entry above and
than the infinity6e entry beside it.

    ssc377/release_0907/sourcecode/project/release/chip/i6c/ipc/common/
      uclibc/9.1.0/modules/5.10/
      glibc/11.1.0/modules/5.10/

Same drop as johnchia/sigmastar-lib's infinity6c 0907 libraries, and the build
stamps agree to the minute -- kernel and userspace halves of one build:

    uclibc  9.1.0   modules build_time.20220907111124   libs 20220907111141
    glibc  11.1.0   modules build_time.20220907112106   libs 20220907112126

Why release_0907 and not the other two SSC377 drops is argued in
sigmastar-lib's infinity6c/PROVENANCE; the same reasoning selects it here.

## Both C library variants are carried

glibc ships three modules uclibc does not:

    mi_jpd.ko  mi_vdec.ko  mi_vdisp.ko

    uclibc  9.1.0   19 modules
    glibc  11.1.0   22 modules

Both are kept because the vendor builds them separately and the md5s differ, so
the path records which tree a module came from. **The difference is far smaller
than the userspace one, and the libc name is close to a misnomer here** --
kernel modules do not link a C library. Measured:

    mi_sys.ko   exports 176 vs 176, identical; imports 235 vs 235, identical
    mi_isp.ko   differs by exactly one import, memmove
    the three glibc-only modules export 0 symbols each

So a build that picks the "wrong" variant is not missing an API -- it gets the
same modules from a different vendor build tree, plus or minus three leaf
modules (JPEG decode, video decode, video display) that nothing on a camera
loads. Contrast sigmastar-lib, where the split is real: glibc there carries
libmi_divp and libsigma3a that uclibc genuinely lacks.

## MI modules only

The vendor's `modules/5.10` holds 143 (uclibc) / 146 (glibc) modules, of which
120-odd are prebuilt sensor drivers. Those are not carried: on this drop sensors
are built from `sensor-src/` as they are for every other family, which is what
keeps them independent of the vendor's kernel build. 1230 above is the exception
and says why.

## Stripped of debug info by this repository

The vendor ships these unstripped -- 14 `.debug*` sections each, 59M (uclibc)
and 37M (glibc) for the MI modules alone. They are carried stripped:

    arm-thingino-linux-gnueabihf-strip --strip-debug mi_*.ko

    uclibc  59M -> 3.7M
    glibc   37M -> 4.0M

Checked rather than assumed, on mi_sys.ko: the exported symbol set is identical
(176 `__ksymtab_*` entries before and after), the imported symbol count is
unchanged at 236, and `vermagic`, `depends=mi_common` and the Sigmastar build
stamp all survive. Only `.debug*` sections are gone.

This is the one place the repository modifies vendor bytes, so the md5s below
are of the stripped files. The originals are recoverable by re-running the strip
above against the vendor path named earlier:

    mi_sys.ko  uclibc   vendor c9e29a1f...  here 2ff20927...
    mi_sys.ko  glibc    vendor 91515d8c...  here 6de03cec...

## 0907's sensor-iq/imx335.bin is not the vendor's

release_0907 has no imx335 tuning -- `board/i6c/iqfile/` holds imx307 and
sc430ai and nothing else -- but `sensor-src/sensor_imx335_mipi.c` is present and
builds, so the driver exists with no blob to pair it with. That copy is
OpenIPC's, from openipc/firmware
`general/package/sigmastar-osdrv-infinity6c/files/sensor/configs/imx335.bin`,
read at e609afae (file last touched 2026-07-17), sha256

    d1139a2ee4f24a7cf1f488fb5450eeb2321102aaab27be10460001d2d2610e0a

    56737fd643c62eedc321a59b3f0436e0  sensor-iq/0907/sc430ai.bin   (vendor)
    c6bf43fd65598621fa5d8c1627bbde35  sensor-iq/0907/imx307.bin    (vendor)
    02a48d0a22f485e608778dc8619e75b2  sensor-iq/0907/imx335.bin    (OpenIPC)

It carries the i6c family tag and the i6c variant byte, matching the two vendor
bins beside it, so it is the right format for this family rather than an i6e file
copied across. What that does not establish is which SDK drop tuned it. OpenIPC's
i6c osdrv is known to mix vintages -- a June-2024 `mi.ko` against release_0907
libraries on a Nov-2023 BSP -- so treat it as plausible and untested. Prefer 1230,
whose imx335 tuning came out of the same image as its modules.

